---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const packageDir = resolve(process.cwd(), '../rikiki');
const pkg = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(packageDir, 'package.json'), 'utf8'));
const nodeRange = pkg.engines.node;
const playwrightRange = pkg.peerDependencies.playwright;
const rolldownRange = pkg.peerDependencies.rolldown;

const installPlaywright = 'npm i -D playwright && npx playwright install chromium';
const installRolldown = 'npm i -D rolldown';

const initCmd = `npx rikiki init index.html

# a single self-contained file instead of a source deck
npx rikiki init index.html --standalone --title "My talk"`;

const checkCmd = `npx rikiki check index.html

# the same report as JSON on stdout, for a script or an agent
npx rikiki check index.html --json

# only the slides you are working on: a number, an id, an interval
npx rikiki check index.html --slides 4
npx rikiki check index.html --slides 2,pricing,6-9`;

const checkOutput = `! TEXT_TOO_SMALL · slide 4 (#pricing)
    text renders at 15px · the back row will not read it
    at  deck-split#pricing > deck-md
    try raise the size, or move the detail into deck-notes

index.html · 12 slide(s) · 0 error(s), 1 warning(s)
Design quality · not-run · NOT VERIFIED`;

const qualityCmd = `# capture every slide and reveal state, and write review/request.json
npx rikiki check index.html --quality-out review

# import the filled-in review; fail unless it passes
npx rikiki check index.html --quality-review review.json --require-quality`;

const renderCmd = `npx rikiki render index.html

# a chosen few, each revealed state, into a directory you name
npx rikiki render index.html --slides 2,pricing --steps --out shots/`;

const bundleCmd = `npx rikiki bundle index.html

# an explicit name, or - to write the file to stdout
npx rikiki bundle index.html talk.html`;

const exportCmd = `npx rikiki export index.html

# choose the file name
npx rikiki export index.html --output talk.pdf`;

const assembleCmd = `npx rikiki assemble deck.config.js`;

const skillsCmd = `npx rikiki skills`;

const sceneCmd = `# the shapes that can say the brief, written and sized
npx rikiki scene plan brief.json

# draw each one in Chromium, keep the plan, the fragments and a picture of each
npx rikiki scene plan brief.json --measure --out plan/

# the slide of the candidate you chose
npx rikiki scene compile brief.json --candidate bars --out slide.html

# how many characters each field of a shape holds, at a width
npx rikiki scene budgets cards --width 752`;

const intentCmd = `# the skeleton of the intent a sentence points at
npx rikiki intent "Six files arrive, review handles two, the rest wait"

# the skeleton of one intent, by name
npx rikiki intent bottleneck

# the sixteen intents, what each demonstrates and what it looks like
npx rikiki intent --list`;

const recipeCmd = `# the twelve recipes, their graphic model and their tempo
npx rikiki recipe --list

# the closest recipe to a sentence · its brief, its sequence, its pitfalls, its skeleton
npx rikiki recipe "Tout est vert, et 19 h sans un lead"

# the example brief as JSON, then your brief drawn as a slide
npx rikiki recipe incident-band --brief > brief.json
npx rikiki recipe incident-band --fill brief.json

# the slide its example brief draws
npx rikiki recipe incident-band --example`;

const sceneBrief = `{
  "schema": "rikiki.scene-brief/1",
  "claim": "The cache cuts the build by two thirds",
  "relation": "compare-size",
  "items": [
    { "label": "No cache", "value": 42 },
    { "label": "Shared cache", "value": 14 }
  ],
  "unit": "min",
  "focus": "Shared cache",
  "source": { "text": "Invented figures", "nature": "illustration" }
}`;
---

<Docs
  title="Check and deliver"
  eyebrow="Guides"
  description="Start a deck, measure it in a real browser, photograph it, and turn it into the file you hand over."
>

<h2 id="what">What the command line is for</h2>
<p>
  A deck is plain HTML, so you can write one and present it without ever
  opening a terminal. The command line covers the three moments where that is
  not enough: starting a deck with its runtime already in place, finding out
  what is wrong with it before a room does, and producing the artefact you
  actually deliver · a single file, a PDF, or a set of pictures.
</p>
<p>
  Two audiences need it. Authors reach for <code>init</code> at the start and
  for <code>export</code> or <code>bundle</code> at the end. Agents and CI jobs
  live on <code>check</code> and <code>render</code>, which are the only two
  commands that report what a deck looks like in a form a program can read.
</p>
<p>
  Every command runs from an install, with no clone and no build step. The
  package declares <code>bin.rikiki</code>, so <code>npx rikiki …</code> works
  as soon as <code>rikiki-deck</code> is a dependency.
</p>

<h2 id="prerequisites">What each command needs</h2>
<p>
  All of them need Node <code>{nodeRange}</code>, the range the package
  declares under <code>engines</code>. Three of them need a browser, as does
  <code>scene plan --measure</code>, and two need a bundler. Both are optional peer dependencies: a deck that only loads
  the runtime never pays for them.
</p>
<table class="opts">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Command</th><th>Beyond Node</th><th>Install line</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#init"><code>init</code></a></td>
      <td>nothing else</td>
      <td>·</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#init"><code>init --standalone</code></a></td>
      <td><code>rolldown</code> {rolldownRange}</td>
      <td><code>{installRolldown}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#check"><code>check</code></a></td>
      <td><code>playwright</code> {playwrightRange}</td>
      <td><code>{installPlaywright}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#render"><code>render</code></a></td>
      <td><code>playwright</code> {playwrightRange}</td>
      <td><code>{installPlaywright}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#bundle"><code>bundle</code></a></td>
      <td><code>rolldown</code> {rolldownRange}</td>
      <td><code>{installRolldown}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#export"><code>export</code></a></td>
      <td><code>playwright</code> {playwrightRange}</td>
      <td><code>{installPlaywright}</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#assemble"><code>assemble</code></a></td>
      <td>nothing else</td>
      <td>·</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#skills"><code>skills</code></a></td>
      <td>nothing else</td>
      <td>·</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#scene"><code>scene</code></a></td>
      <td>nothing else</td>
      <td>·</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="#scene"><code>scene plan --measure</code></a></td>
      <td><code>playwright</code> {playwrightRange}</td>
      <td><code>{installPlaywright}</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  A missing peer is reported as a sentence with the line that installs it, not
  as a stack trace.
</p>

<h2 id="init">init · start a deck</h2>
<p>
  Writes a deck you can edit, plus the runtime it loads, next to it.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={initCmd} />
<p>
  The default form produces <code>index.html</code> and copies
  <code>dist</code>, <code>tokens.css</code>, <code>themes</code> and
  <code>fonts</code> into <code>rikiki/</code> beside it. Serve that folder over
  <code>http://</code>; ES modules do not load from <code>file://</code>.
</p>
<p>
  With <code>--standalone</code> you get one self-contained file instead, and the
  command exits non-zero if that file would still fetch anything at runtime. A
  second <code>init</code> on an existing name refuses to overwrite unless you
  pass <code>--force</code>.
</p>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>--standalone</code></td><td>emit one self-contained file instead of a source deck</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--title "…"</code></td><td>deck title · defaults to the file name</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--theme rikiki|siliceum</code></td><td>starting theme · a source deck copies <code>fonts/</code> either way, and under <code>--standalone</code> only <code>siliceum</code> has local fonts to inline (<code>rikiki</code> reads its typefaces from Google Fonts)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--with-mermaid</code>, <code>--with-shiki</code></td><td>also carry the mermaid runtime or the Shiki highlighter</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-fonts</code></td><td>drop the fonts rather than inline them</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--force</code></td><td>overwrite a deck that is already there</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="check">check · measure the deck</h2>
<p>
  Loads the deck in a real browser, measures it, and reports each finding with
  a stable code, the slide it belongs to and the number that justifies it.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={checkCmd} />
<p>
  The human report goes to stderr, one finding at a time, and ends with a
  count of errors and warnings.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="stderr · an example report" code={checkOutput} />

<h3 id="check-exit">Exit codes</h3>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>the deck was measured and no finding has error severity</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>the deck was measured and at least one finding is an error</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>the deck could not be read at all · the path is missing or is not a file</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  The split matters to whoever calls the command: <code>1</code> says fix the
  deck, <code>2</code> says fix the call.
</p>

<h3 id="check-json">The JSON report</h3>
<p>
  With <code>--json</code>, stdout carries the report and nothing else, so it
  pipes without stripping anything · including when the deck is broken. Notes
  stay on stderr. The report is versioned by a <code>schema</code> field and
  carries the deck name, the slide count, the thresholds that were applied, an
  <code>error</code> and <code>warning</code> summary, the diagnostics, and a
  <code>notChecked</code> list naming what nobody looked at.
</p>

<h3 id="check-review">Review the design and the story</h3>
<p>
  Measurements do not approve a slide. The last line of the report says so: the
  design stays <code>NOT VERIFIED</code> until someone has looked at every
  screenshot. <code>--quality-out</code> captures each slide and each reveal
  state, then writes a <code>request.json</code> with a review template: six
  criteria per state (hierarchy, typography, readability, composition, visual
  evidence, theme coherence), each needing a verdict and the visible evidence
  behind it.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={qualityCmd} />
<p>
  A review is tied to the deck it describes: edit the deck and the old review
  is refused as stale. Every failed criterion becomes an error diagnostic
  (<code>QUALITY_HIERARCHY</code>, <code>QUALITY_TYPOGRAPHY</code>, and so on)
  and must carry a concrete correction.
</p>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>--quality-out &lt;dir&gt;</code></td><td>capture every state and prepare the design review</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--quality-review &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>import a completed screenshot-based review</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--require-quality</code></td><td>add a <code>QUALITY_REQUIRED</code> error until an imported review is complete</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--narrative-out &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>prepare the material for a review of the story, slide by slide</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--narrative-review &lt;file&gt;</code></td><td>import that structured review</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="check-categories">What it looks at</h3>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Codes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>the deck never ran</td>
      <td><code>RUNTIME_NOT_LOADED</code>, <code>NO_DECK_ROOT</code>, <code>NO_SLIDES</code>, <code>PAGE_ERROR</code>, <code>RESOURCE_MISSING</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>markup that renders as nothing</td>
      <td><code>UNKNOWN_ELEMENT</code>, <code>STRAY_MARKUP</code>, <code>CONTENT_NOT_RENDERED</code>, <code>UNKNOWN_ATTRIBUTE</code>, <code>DUPLICATE_SLIDE_ID</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>what fits on the slide</td>
      <td><code>CONTENT_CLIPPED</code>, <code>SLIDE_DENSE</code>, <code>SLIDE_TOP_HEAVY</code>, <code>TEXT_TOO_SMALL</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>diagram geometry</td>
      <td><code>GRAPH_NODE_OUT_OF_BOUNDS</code>, <code>GRAPH_EDGE_CROSSES_NODE</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>what a diagram demonstrates</td>
      <td>the <code>INT_*</code> findings of a diagram written as an <a href="#intent">intent</a> · errors, each with its measurement and what was expected</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>illustrations that look alike</td>
      <td><code>ILL_REPEATED_LOOK</code> · a warning about the deck, never an error of a slide</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>the talk and what it pulls in</td>
      <td><code>TALK_SHORTER_THAN_ANNOUNCED</code>, <code>EXTERNAL_DEPENDENCY</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  <code>SLIDE_TOP_HEAVY</code> comes from a pixel pass that takes one
  screenshot per slide and measures where the ink sits.
  <code>--no-visual</code> skips that pass; the report then says the pixels
  were not measured. <code>--width</code> and <code>--height</code> set the
  canvas the deck is measured against.
</p>

<h3 id="check-diversity">Illustrations that look alike</h3>
<p>
  A deck can pass every rule slide by slide and still show one picture thirty
  times with other words. <code>check</code> compares its illustrations with
  each other, on what they are made of: their structure (the intent, the
  layouts, the kinds of marks, how the arrows connect), their look (surfaces,
  tones, a coarse thumbnail of the ink) and their time (clicks, what moves).
  The distance is <code>0.5 structure + 0.3 visual + 0.2 time</code>; two
  drawings closer than 0.15 are <code>ILL_REPEATED_LOOK</code>. The report adds
  a <code>diversity</code> line and object (distinct structures, intents
  covered, the largest family, the pairs alike), and
  <code>--diversity</code> adds every distance and each drawing's nearest
  neighbour.
</p>

<h3 id="check-richness">Illustrations with something to see</h3>
<p>
  A clean, varied drawing can still say nothing: three pale boxes named
  « Service A », « Service B », « Service C ». Each illustration of the report
  carries a <code>richness</code> out of 100, on five criteria of 20 points:
  <code>content</code> (two precise facts of two kinds, when, which and how
  much, and a <code>deck-source</code> on the slide), <code>demonstration</code>
  (two devices of relation: a link, a flow, a gate, a scale, a marker, a
  callout, a state that turns), <code>story</code> (the system running, the
  break, the proof after it), <code>paint</code> (as many flat fills as roles,
  up to three) and <code>result</code> (a lasting proof, or the figure in the
  thing put forward). Each gives its measurement and how to earn the rest. A
  criterion that does not apply is left out: a still of a share is not judged
  as a story. More marks, colours or seconds earn nothing.
</p>
<p>
  It is a measure, not a verdict: no score changes and nothing fails. The
  report adds a <code>Richness</code> line; <code>--richness</code> prints each
  drawing's points and says three warnings, <code>ILL_GENERIC</code> (no
  precise fact), <code>ILL_FLAT_PAINT</code> (roles painted alike) and
  <code>ILL_MODEL_REPEAT</code> (the same graphic model a third time in five
  illustrated slides, unless its named marks keep their place and their words
  move on). Measured on the story decks, they average 95; the galleries written
  before them, 61; pictures poor on purpose, 20.
</p>

<h3 id="check-slides">Checking only some slides</h3>
<p>
  <code>--slides</code> measures the slides you name and leaves the others
  alone, which turns a minute-long run on a long deck into a few seconds while
  you work on one slide. It takes the same list as <code>render</code>:
  a 1-based number (<code>3</code>), a slide id (<code>pricing</code>), an
  interval (<code>4-9</code>, <code>-3</code> for the first three,
  <code>8-</code> for the last ones) or two ends joined by <code>..</code>
  (<code>intro..pricing</code>, each end a number or an id), separated by
  commas. The report keeps the whole deck's <code>slideCount</code>, lists the
  positions it measured in <code>slidesChecked</code>, and says in
  <code>notChecked</code> that the other slides were not looked at. A name that
  matches no slide stops before anything is measured, names what the deck
  holds, and exits with <code>2</code>. Run the whole deck, without the flag,
  before you ship.
</p>

<h3 id="check-policy">Choosing the rules · for the deck, some slides, some elements</h3>
<p>
  Every rule has a code (<code>TEXT_TOO_SMALL</code>) and a name
  (<code>text-too-small</code>); <code>--list-checks</code> prints them all,
  whether the deck keeps each and why. The policy lives in
  <code>rikiki.config.json</code>, under <code>check</code>: every such file from
  the deck's folder up applies, outermost first, until one that says
  <code>"root": true</code> or the repository's root. <code>checks</code> drops or
  takes back rules in order (<code>-slide-dense</code>, <code>-ill-*</code>,
  <code>-*,content-*</code>), <code>severity</code> sets one to
  <code>error</code>, <code>warning</code> or <code>off</code>,
  <code>warningsAsErrors</code> fails the run on some warnings,
  <code>limits</code> moves a threshold (<code>minTextPx</code>,
  <code>ill.words</code>), and <code>overrides</code> do the same for the slides,
  the kinds of slide, the tagged slides (<code>data-tags="annexe"</code>) or the
  elements (a CSS selector) they name. On the command line,
  <code>--checks</code>, <code>--severity rule=level</code>,
  <code>--warnings-as-errors</code>, <code>--limit name=value</code> and
  <code>--check-option CODE.option=value</code> speak after the files.
</p>
<p>
  A deck may write an exception on itself, which only silences a warning and
  only with its reason:
  <code>data-check-off="slide-dense" data-check-reason="grille contractuelle"</code>
  on <code>deck-root</code>, a slide or an element.
  <code>--no-inline-exceptions</code> ignores them all, for a CI. Nothing is lost:
  what the policy silences is listed under <code>suppressed</code> with what
  silenced it and why (<code>--show-suppressed</code> in the human report), an
  exception without a reason, naming no rule or silencing nothing is said, and
  a page that threw or a file that did not load is reported whatever the policy
  says. The reference shipped with the package
  (<code>docs/llms/rikiki-reference.md</code>, « A check policy ») gives every key.
</p>

<h3 id="check-limits">What it does not test</h3>
<p>
  The report is a set of measurements, not a verdict on the deck, and it says
  so in its own <code>notChecked</code> list:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>accessibility · no contrast, focus order or screen-reader check is run.</li>
  <li>revealed steps · only the opening state of each slide is measured.</li>
  <li>wording, facts and figures · nothing here reads the content.</li>
  <li>design quality · judged only by a <a href="#check-review">screenshot review</a> you import.</li>
  <li>other viewports · the deck is measured at its own canvas size.</li>
  <li>text inside a diagram · an SVG scales by its viewBox, which is not measured.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="render">render · one picture per slide</h2>
<p>
  Photographs the deck so that something without eyes on the screen, a review
  thread or an agent, can look at it.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={renderCmd} />
<p>
  The pictures land in <code>index.shots/</code> unless <code>--out</code>
  names another directory. Beside them sit a gallery page,
  <code>index.html</code>, that opens offline from a file manager, and a
  <code>manifest.json</code> that ties every picture back to the slide it came
  from: its index, its id, its tag, its title and its file name. That manifest
  is what turns "the third picture" into an element you can edit.
</p>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>--out</code> <em>dir</em></td><td>where the pictures go · defaults to the deck name plus <code>.shots</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--slides</code> <em>a,b</em></td><td>which slides · 1-based numbers, slide ids, intervals (<code>4-9</code>, <code>-3</code>, <code>8-</code>) or two ends (<code>intro..pricing</code>), in deck order · an unknown name is an error · <code>rikiki check</code> takes the same list</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--steps</code></td><td>one picture per revealed state · without it a stepped slide is caught in its opening state</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--width</code>, <code>--height</code></td><td>canvas size in pixels</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="bundle">bundle · one file to send</h2>
<p>
  Folds an existing deck, its theme, its runtime and its images into a single
  HTML file with no external reference.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={bundleCmd} />
<p>
  Without a second argument the file lands next to the deck as
  <code>index.bundle.html</code>; <code>-</code> writes it to stdout instead.
  The bundle is curated to the components the deck uses and to the icons it
  draws, and the HTML and CSS stay readable so the result is still editable.
  If anything in the produced file would still be fetched at runtime, the
  command says what and exits non-zero · a bundle that fetches is a broken
  deliverable, not a warning.
</p>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>--with-mermaid</code>, <code>--with-shiki</code></td><td>inline the mermaid runtime or the Shiki highlighter · needed when the deck uses them</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--all</code></td><td>carry every component instead of the ones the deck uses</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--include</code> <em>a,b</em></td><td>force-include components that only appear from JS</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-fonts</code></td><td>drop the fonts and fall back to system ones</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--minify-css</code>, <code>--minify-html</code></td><td>trade readability for size</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-minify-js</code></td><td>keep the framework JS readable</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="export">export · a PDF, one page per slide</h2>
<p>
  Prints the deck for the people who asked for the slides afterwards.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={exportCmd} />
<p>
  The file lands as <code>index.pdf</code> unless <code>--output</code> (or
  <code>-o</code>) names another one. Page geometry and page breaks come from
  the deck's own print stylesheet, backgrounds are printed, and the PDF is
  tagged with one outline entry per slide so a reader can jump around. Anything
  the deck could not load is listed before the file is announced, because a
  page that printed without an image is worth saying out loud.
</p>

<h2 id="assemble">assemble · one deck from many files</h2>
<p>
  Joins ordered partials into the single deck file the rest of the commands
  expect.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={assembleCmd} />
<p>
  The config lists a title, a theme href, a runtime href and the ordered
  parts; <code>.html</code> parts are inlined as they are and <code>.md</code>
  parts split into slides. The output is a normal deck, so
  <code>check</code>, <code>render</code>, <code>bundle</code> and
  <code>export</code> all take it. A second argument names the output file, and
  <code>-</code> sends it to stdout. See
  <a href="/docs/multi-deck">Multi-file decks</a> for the manifest and the
  partial formats.
</p>

<h2 id="skills">skills · the agent skills</h2>
<p>
  Copies the skills shipped with the package into a project, so a coding agent
  discovers them on its own.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={skillsCmd} />
<p>
  The installed skills are <code>rikiki-deck</code>,
  <code>rikiki-theme</code>, <code>rikiki-debug</code>,
  <code>rikiki-illustrate</code> and <code>rikiki-scenes</code>. They land in
  <code>.claude/skills/</code> unless <code>--dir</code> names another
  location, an existing skill is left alone unless you pass
  <code>--force</code>, and the command reports what it wrote.
</p>

<h2 id="scene">scene · from a brief to a slide</h2>
<p>
  Turns what a slide must prove into the animated scenes that can show it. You
  write a brief, a JSON file that names the claim, the relation between the
  items (<code>parallel</code>, <code>sequence</code>,
  <code>compare-size</code>, <code>before-after</code>…), the figures and their
  source. The shape follows from the relation, never the other way round.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="brief.json" lang="js" code={sceneBrief} />
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={sceneCmd} />
<p>
  <code>rikiki scene plan</code> validates the brief, then lists each candidate
  with its shape, whether it fits, the modules it loads and what the scene
  lint and the measured character table say about it, followed by the shapes
  it left out and why. Nothing here calls a model: the same brief always gives
  the same plan, and choosing a candidate stays with you.
  With <code>--measure</code>, each candidate is also drawn in Chromium in the
  theme's fonts (<code>--theme rikiki|siliceum</code>), its height and the
  problems the scenes report once drawn are added under <code>measured</code>,
  a picture is taken, and a candidate with an error found that way no longer
  fits. <code>--out</code> writes <code>candidates.json</code>, one
  <code>&lt;id&gt;.html</code> fragment and, when measured, one
  <code>&lt;id&gt;.png</code> per candidate.
</p>
<p>
  <code>rikiki scene compile</code> writes the whole slide of the candidate you
  name: the claim as its title, the scene, the source and the notes, headed by
  a comment that lists the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags to add once to the
  deck. <code>rikiki scene budgets</code> prints the narrowest width a shape
  draws cleanly at, and how many characters each field holds for each number
  of items.
</p>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>--json</code></td><td>plan, budgets: the result on stdout and nothing else, an invalid brief included</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--out</code> <em>dir</em></td><td>plan: where the plan, the fragments and the pictures go · compile: the file the slide is written to</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--measure</code></td><td>plan: draw and photograph each candidate · without <code>--out</code> the pictures go to a temporary directory, named on stderr</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--candidate</code> <em>id</em></td><td>compile: the candidate to write · an unknown one is refused with the list of those the plan has</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--width</code> <em>units</em></td><td>budgets: the scene's width in drawing units (default 1600, the whole canvas)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<table class="opts">
  <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>plan: at least one candidate fits · compile, budgets: done</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>plan: no candidate fits</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>the call cannot be carried out · a brief missing, not JSON or not valid (each error is listed by its path), an unknown candidate or shape</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="intent">intent · from a sentence to a diagram that proves it</h2>
<p>
  Prints the skeleton of a <code>deck-diagram</code> written as an intent: the
  relation the picture demonstrates, one of sixteen (<code>cause</code>,
  <code>barrier</code>, <code>bottleneck</code>, <code>accumulate</code>,
  <code>transform</code>, <code>feedback</code>, <code>scale</code>,
  <code>contain</code>, <code>parallel</code>, <code>propagate</code>,
  <code>threshold</code>, <code>tradeoff</code>, <code>drift</code>,
  <code>compare</code>, <code>partition</code>, <code>merge</code>), and the
  part each mark plays in it (<code>role="…"</code>). The intent chooses the
  layout, the one thing put forward and the order things come in, each its own
  way, so two intents never draw alike; the skeleton holds no coordinate and no
  duration.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={intentCmd} />
<p>
  Given a sentence, it looks for the cues of each intent in it (it cuts, it
  waits, it piles up, it loops, it spreads, past a line, at the cost of, before
  and after) and prints the closest one; nothing calls a model, so the same
  sentence always gives the same skeleton. Above the diagram, a comment lists
  its roles with their bounds, the intents it composes with
  (<code>also="…"</code>) or why it takes none, and the mistakes
  <code>rikiki check</code> rejects, the <code>INT_*</code> findings: a gate
  that leaves a lane out, a neck as wide as what feeds it, a loop that never
  comes back, a crowd under ten times its unit, parts that do not add up to the
  whole, a wave that jumps a rank, a gap that does not widen. A defect of
  meaning is an error, whatever the drawing's score. <code>--list</code> names
  the sixteen intents with what each demonstrates and what it looks like. A
  sentence that tells a story rather than a relation (an incident, a remedy, a
  zoom out) exits with <code>3</code> and names the closest
  <a href="#recipe">recipes</a>; a sentence with no cue at all exits with
  <code>1</code> and names the intents. With an intent found, the comment also
  names the recipes near it.
</p>

<h2 id="recipe">recipe · a graphic model, filled from a brief</h2>
<p>
  Prints one of twelve recipes: a skeleton of <code>deck-diagram</code> told as a
  story played on entry, taken from a graphic model (incident frise, lanes and a gate, a probe, wiring, a switch-over, a
  race, records through a filter, lanes of versions, an escalation, a pulse and
  its threshold, rings of scope, a replay of data). Its marks are named for the
  part they play, its <code>{'{{fields}}'}</code> are the facts of a brief and its
  beats are the sequence the model imposes. Above the skeleton, a comment lists
  the brief to fill (each field, what it holds, an example), the sequence, what
  <code>rikiki check</code> guarantees, when not to use it, its neighbours and
  its pitfalls.
</p>
<CodeBlock label="terminal" lang="js" code={recipeCmd} />
<p>
  The brief comes first: the real system and its actors, its normal pace, the
  dated event, the exact cause, the consequence counted, the proof.
  <code>--fill</code> refuses a brief (exit <code>1</code>, each field said) when a
  field is empty, generic, longer than its place holds, or when the brief holds
  fewer than two precise values (a date, an hour, a number, an identifier).
  Like <code>intent</code>, a sentence is read by its cues and nothing calls a
  model: the same sentence always gives the same recipe.
</p>

